[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180031 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179644 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b7b8782-d1f4-5654-a18f-6ad35de8fb30" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Flux's Sign From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58f54ef5-437d-5af8-a751-b33cd1dcdd2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459517} {:answer/id #uuid "5ca4da76-b626-5282-9e3e-c1842c95aac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459518}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ca4da76-b626-5282-9e3e-c1842c95aac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459518} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1ea5b52-19ab-545d-b803-91faf4690e76" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460625} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a9ad46d-5411-5071-a050-a7cba8cd0ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459519} {:answer/id #uuid "e615d0a2-c730-5dba-9263-c8a081802791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a9ad46d-5411-5071-a050-a7cba8cd0ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459519} :answer-field/id #uuid "743e0df3-174a-5257-a160-51980afa07d4" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460626} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "021ca85e-dc58-5e81-8883-f8dafe828184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459521} {:answer/id #uuid "2081d764-18e3-59ca-9a1b-b5feb261819b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2081d764-18e3-59ca-9a1b-b5feb261819b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459522} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b26c2a4-4801-5c19-84fb-9b2aa432ffa6" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2d693ae-0e11-561b-9b69-4ee74bfcc078" :question/math-academy-id "q-126726" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e679efaa2612d45bbfe8b918de7148853b64eaf530af34a525ef1735e0946b09.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive” is true, while statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero” is false. From the diagram below it's clear that there is a net flow *out* through $C.$ \n\n![](images/fb/fbcd6fd0fadcc955212a145de9edb4654ddb92ddebb8ba31eb775cff676efeeb.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive” is true. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/a3/a3d398d382e6bc94ddde77e650d50a6b5ec050083f89f64104f6f2fd172a9239.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive”; “The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180032 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179644 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b7b8782-d1f4-5654-a18f-6ad35de8fb30" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Flux's Sign From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10e48c47-8ee2-519f-87f2-b570d3b8dba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459523} {:answer/id #uuid "cc34336b-2e10-5376-83aa-f53156f87b43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10e48c47-8ee2-519f-87f2-b570d3b8dba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459523} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e423910-3dd4-5ab2-a129-1141a47b6bb0" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460628} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bc0bec0-62de-5062-ba34-89678d7a79ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459525} {:answer/id #uuid "970b48b7-8b9c-59fe-a14e-26cd96ab46a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459526}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "970b48b7-8b9c-59fe-a14e-26cd96ab46a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459526} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea2eff81-f7dd-5d77-8c8f-1eb3b01feb94" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460629} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e835fdd-dbbb-5253-a963-0bad2097d16f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459527} {:answer/id #uuid "2d95a79c-9182-55b2-8789-e4ef4cdec237" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e835fdd-dbbb-5253-a963-0bad2097d16f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459527} :answer-field/id #uuid "755d3cce-0252-5c3d-be63-5bdb7c00b632" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "505ec5b2-1b42-5150-bddf-6e7219956cd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-126728" :question/problem "![](images/77/772added7ce46ba0ec25de66dfd03e0107787d851006f261ac8843eba008a2d2.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive” is false, while statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero” is true. From the diagram below we see that $F$ acts perpendicular to $n$ at every point on $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $F$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/d7/d7a469a331a3cda238fcefb9e7163743483975c4ecc7b962d6d5f8d79736693c.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative” is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/29/29f144d6f3ce8233a034025fe933bc57dbea5476c50a259853a98bd2a2b3a47b.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180084 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179766 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc613be0-2d34-5253-8fbe-7d2a5fcd0840" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Cases Where a Stream Function Exists"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2ec7aa8-dd39-59f0-9c72-cd2b71897203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477159} {:answer/id #uuid "ecba3797-1b79-59e1-94cf-45937dc83664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecba3797-1b79-59e1-94cf-45937dc83664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477160} :answer-field/id #uuid "e41a182b-f440-53a0-a770-6b1b1f8ee187" :answer-field/key "$F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477549} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3b05c34-4850-52cc-b19c-672abb4a9748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477161} {:answer/id #uuid "073a9b12-3569-542b-90dc-67ecc19682c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3b05c34-4850-52cc-b19c-672abb4a9748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477161} :answer-field/id #uuid "8255f2e6-c5ca-5d62-a67a-bf22d30dd31f" :answer-field/key "$F(x,y)=xy\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477550} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66a622b6-7c56-5c1d-8127-475db45665ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477163} {:answer/id #uuid "38009be4-58e5-583a-827a-bf14ac36798b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66a622b6-7c56-5c1d-8127-475db45665ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477163} :answer-field/id #uuid "b47f7fc9-bc5c-5be1-9460-c233b25d60e2" :answer-field/key "$F(x,y)=2x\\,i-y\\,j$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3be31acc-b938-5628-8e53-a18ffa022892" :question/math-academy-id "q-161436" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0+0=0\n$$\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=xy\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$ doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=y+2y\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=2x\\,i-y\\,j$ doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2+(-1)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184603 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184151 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f3ba8a2-f374-5ff0-8d46-b1e4cc49e3c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459731} {:answer/id #uuid "d9f4725e-06fa-5bb9-8df0-53060c59bc67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459732}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9f4725e-06fa-5bb9-8df0-53060c59bc67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459732} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9f1976f-f067-5f01-8186-d3d93e10c9a1" :answer-field/key "$f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460732} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1d621ec-c2b1-5ad2-82cb-bb412ad83365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459733} {:answer/id #uuid "19a8ec59-5411-56da-8ec9-e91bef5ecdf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1d621ec-c2b1-5ad2-82cb-bb412ad83365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459733} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0fa5f92-f5e8-5072-9751-731861433a89" :answer-field/key "$f(x)$ is concave down on $(-\\infty ,0)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460733} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37c26edb-fe62-5bab-936d-163bf45dbd60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459735} {:answer/id #uuid "1fe8759d-2a68-56ae-b5ab-81b73af5bad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fe8759d-2a68-56ae-b5ab-81b73af5bad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459736} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e07c1f5-403e-5e7b-a75c-b5c5379cc5da" :answer-field/key "$f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\infty )$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d75ab107-adf9-5afd-9401-bb2be46187e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-22703" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}-2{e}^{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x-2{e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2-2{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-2{e}^{x} & >0 \\\\ {e}^{x} & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y={e}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/c1/c199b110a3d19ecc23454f52dc7470af318ce871805595fd5fe7609dee5b0545.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that ${e}^{x}<1$ when $x<0.$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(-\\infty ,0).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality ${e}^{x}>1,$ which has the solution $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0)$”; “$f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\infty )$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184151 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc9224b4-433a-5f80-a270-42e5f6c8f479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459737} {:answer/id #uuid "75eef7c3-8ef6-538f-b899-cfbfca2092bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc9224b4-433a-5f80-a270-42e5f6c8f479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459737} :answer-field/id #uuid "0273e3d2-8f40-57b0-8760-87a6e4708855" :answer-field/key "$f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460735} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e88db70-4c40-5b96-8eeb-76200ab3e765" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459739} {:answer/id #uuid "a4af2a11-d55c-5902-a757-e674f4fa1006" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e88db70-4c40-5b96-8eeb-76200ab3e765" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459739} :answer-field/id #uuid "feec5bc4-700c-509a-9a99-e729bc37944f" :answer-field/key "$f(x)$ is concave up on $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460736} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f6278ba-04dc-5eaa-8a03-374d43ff3e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459741} {:answer/id #uuid "e4f83e2f-da64-5b8f-9e71-f3ab0371c271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459742}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4f83e2f-da64-5b8f-9e71-f3ab0371c271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459742} :answer-field/id #uuid "57e62459-e37c-51f5-af05-d2e3cb154f78" :answer-field/key "$f(x)$ is concave down on $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7adfc0dd-472e-52b3-a8a1-63de79ba74b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-22809" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ defined on $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & >0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ over the interval $(0,\\pi ).$\n\n![](images/41/410e0af2ed20a065c27da5edaf0095dfd22c8a37a92e3d8fbaeece629cefa278.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0$ when $0<x<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ which has the solution $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)$ is concave down on $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184875 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184197 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8063bc9c-a10d-562b-93b1-508b4b26a674" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Singular Values of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a89b920-2812-5e9a-b4ef-b55725fa54c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477485} {:answer/id #uuid "6ad7705e-e7ec-5600-a568-52b823d8bf79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a89b920-2812-5e9a-b4ef-b55725fa54c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477485} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f0ff7a5-9d78-5132-81a7-073af4ec9870" :answer-field/key "Any $3\\times 2$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477712} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65778103-abb1-5a51-ba9a-c549e5e3ad81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477487} {:answer/id #uuid "7d190e4c-24a2-525e-9cd4-0c565240aa00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65778103-abb1-5a51-ba9a-c549e5e3ad81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477487} :answer-field/id #uuid "c241f598-5ae6-546e-8c3c-ce84c20b1525" :answer-field/key "The matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ has one negative singular value." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477713} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2c301e8-3042-5b83-9d6f-a3cd3c672e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477489} {:answer/id #uuid "648690a7-b25d-5331-97c9-37c91ce063f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "648690a7-b25d-5331-97c9-37c91ce063f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477490} :answer-field/id #uuid "fad352fd-d9d6-51a8-ba50-39d9fde26b2e" :answer-field/key "Any $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b18f54c-8c0b-56f3-8034-8d24cc9bdc28" :question/math-academy-id "q-94772" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement “Any $3\\times 2$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values” is false. Since $A$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $2\\times 2$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{2\\times 3}\\times {A}_{3\\times 2}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $2$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement “The matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ has one negative singular value” is false. All singular values of any matrix are non-negative. So, $A$ can't have a negative singular value.\n\n- Statement “Any $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values” is true. Since $A$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{3\\times 2}\\times {A}_{2\\times 3}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{3\\times 3}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “Any $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184880 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184197 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8063bc9c-a10d-562b-93b1-508b4b26a674" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Singular Values of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8efaa7d5-f9c1-56d1-b041-870f83717eff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448816} {:answer/id #uuid "d28105b0-23f3-5732-9a82-7db8b4e10ae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d28105b0-23f3-5732-9a82-7db8b4e10ae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448817} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bc10bda-7ab1-5fd3-896c-4bee40367151" :answer-field/key "Any $6\\times 8$ matrix $A$ has at most $8$ distinct singular values" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449448} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ecbf0cb-7482-525b-af23-0f6eedf07bf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448818} {:answer/id #uuid "75f995d4-8d71-5835-90af-2cf0d9e4393d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ecbf0cb-7482-525b-af23-0f6eedf07bf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448818} :answer-field/id #uuid "39e6f456-10c0-5895-9297-e441c42c23b7" :answer-field/key "Any $10\\times 8$ matrix $A$ has at most $10$ distinct singular values." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449449} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fde610d7-4aa6-5762-bc9a-8f3a82dec1ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448820} {:answer/id #uuid "3cfbd6db-1b8f-5c35-858d-8ef464e8ed9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cfbd6db-1b8f-5c35-858d-8ef464e8ed9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448821} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0ff5f73-3abd-58c8-a261-ec9d90c47add" :answer-field/key "The matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has two non-negative singular values." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca5e4c86-b607-523c-a857-24250b844373" :question/math-academy-id "q-95299" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement “Any $6\\times 8$ matrix $A$ has at most $8$ distinct singular values” is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $6\\times 8$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $8\\times 6$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $8\\times 8$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{8\\times 6}\\times {A}_{6\\times 8}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{8\\times 8}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $8$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement “Any $10\\times 8$ matrix $A$ has at most $10$ distinct singular values” is false. Since $A$ is a $10\\times 8$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $8\\times 10$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $8\\times 8$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{8\\times 10}\\times {A}_{10\\times 8}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{8\\times 8}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $8$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement “The matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has two non-negative singular values” is true.  Indeed, all singular values of any matrix are non-negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “Any $6\\times 8$ matrix $A$ has at most $8$ distinct singular values”; “The matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has two non-negative singular values” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188911 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e12609b7-286b-50a8-8373-a331d60ab904" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef4c3240-efcc-5031-be97-b18f24fa624c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477283} {:answer/id #uuid "80eef841-b3bb-539f-a20d-b7af5fdc129f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef4c3240-efcc-5031-be97-b18f24fa624c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477283} :answer-field/id #uuid "97b22468-5364-5814-ab56-00d3827125a4" :answer-field/key "$\\text{rank}(T)=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477611} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ddb04cf-498a-5aa6-bd12-7e6b3799bde4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477285} {:answer/id #uuid "6462c36b-1577-5e5f-980f-afbd1f21ace1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6462c36b-1577-5e5f-980f-afbd1f21ace1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477286} :answer-field/id #uuid "f417e3d1-8fa9-576f-97f4-84f0c7ed2417" :answer-field/key "$\\text{nullity}(T)=2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477612} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7971526-d720-5bf2-916b-1c3268cfc3a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477287} {:answer/id #uuid "ca68341f-f266-5714-a2d3-03c3de4d3096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7971526-d720-5bf2-916b-1c3268cfc3a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477287} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf2f78e9-40f7-51c7-9823-236bb1c047ce" :answer-field/key "$T$ is a bijection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e86ac40c-9f96-593f-88c6-aef65bbc3b17" :question/math-academy-id "q-38136" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and let ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ be a set of linearly independent vectors in ${R}^{5}.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If  $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$, then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}.$ *\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=5.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ is false. Since $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ and the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, we get that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=3.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$ is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =5-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =5-3 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is a bijection” is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 5$ (or since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$), the linear transformation $T$ cannot be a bijection according to the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188925 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e12609b7-286b-50a8-8373-a331d60ab904" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "140e8026-dca4-518e-b2e3-6a5204699c9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448696} {:answer/id #uuid "91598bc4-014d-5d6b-96f5-a46fe7a19ef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91598bc4-014d-5d6b-96f5-a46fe7a19ef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448697} :answer-field/id #uuid "b185c70a-936c-56ce-a09e-491c0f5906d5" :answer-field/key "If $\\begin{gathered}\\text{Im}(T)=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}\\,[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\\\\\,3x+2y-z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449388} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f7fce4d-3fa7-56db-8465-e02eebbb3df7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448698} {:answer/id #uuid "973d8243-55d9-5ba6-9a8d-9dc8eca9d0e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f7fce4d-3fa7-56db-8465-e02eebbb3df7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448698} :answer-field/id #uuid "6276cdd8-8c2d-5458-9242-9db53bf98878" :answer-field/key "If ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are two different solutions to $Tx=[1,-2,1]^{T}$ then $T$ is invertible" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449389} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8433f050-2198-5d04-bcdc-9660fdcf23e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448700} {:answer/id #uuid "3c8ade48-b616-5c30-ace3-11755e23e2b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8433f050-2198-5d04-bcdc-9660fdcf23e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448700} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bfb10db-820c-5886-b42e-f40a5038d34d" :answer-field/key "If ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the columns of $T$ and ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}+2{v}_{2}$, then $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1b3a8f74-a4d2-5020-9ea6-4077acac1b2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-47524" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $\\begin{gathered}\\text{Im}(T)=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}\\,[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\\\\\,3x+2y-z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$” is true. Solving $3x+2y-z=0$ for $z,$ we get $z=3x+2y.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ 3x+2y\\end{bmatrix}=x\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{1}}+y\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are two different solutions to $Tx=[1,-2,1]^{T}$ then $T$ is invertible” is false. If ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are two different solutions to $Tx=[1,-2,1]^{T},$ then \n\n$$\nT{v}_{1}=T{v}_{2}\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}$ is a non-zero vector. This means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Null}(T)\\ne {0}$ and, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $T$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement “If ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the columns of $T$ and ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}+2{v}_{2}$, then $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$” is false. If the columns of $T$ are such that ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}+2{v}_{2},$ then the set of vectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent and therefore $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Now, according to the matrix invertible theorem, we obtain that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “If $\\begin{gathered}\\text{Im}(T)=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}\\,[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\\\\\,3x+2y-z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192944 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7adf2c3e-4a73-5098-ab60-790d637728cf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Open and Closed Sets in the Plane in Set Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57d2ca69-fe95-54f0-aad8-443617662a20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459347} {:answer/id #uuid "de690490-72bf-5c90-a293-08435eace5a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de690490-72bf-5c90-a293-08435eace5a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459348} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7e355aa-15a4-5a22-b9e5-e88f52e20aae" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\\\\\,1\\le y\\le 4\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460540} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66c9609f-e55a-5da3-a3dd-a8cd7b2f8d3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459349} {:answer/id #uuid "bc157483-6944-5715-8eb9-987ffd92b338" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66c9609f-e55a-5da3-a3dd-a8cd7b2f8d3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459349} :answer-field/id #uuid "7613d32b-c5ca-5ef1-9d27-8bbf082c4ac4" :answer-field/key "$\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<5\\}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460541} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f1b039b-b084-53b0-96f0-7271425ca3ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459351} {:answer/id #uuid "822ec6e5-7d2e-5a73-b1e9-337eefabcaf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "822ec6e5-7d2e-5a73-b1e9-337eefabcaf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459352} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fcbcd2d-e013-5f8c-a9fa-537b5994c968" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le x+1,\\\\\\,-1\\le x\\le 1\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "05445495-e773-5bcb-8190-bfc30cf9688f" :question/math-academy-id "q-105211" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are closed?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\\\\\,1\\le y\\le 4\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$; $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le x+1,\\\\\\,-1\\le x\\le 1\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "441a1de0-dc64-59fe-99a8-0ab502841425" :knowledge-point/title "Checking Whether a Given Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c07ee77-6bcc-5b50-ae7f-263f1e68ebb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448398} {:answer/id #uuid "d6935de6-83e9-57e4-ac80-cba94866793a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6935de6-83e9-57e4-ac80-cba94866793a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448399} :answer-field/id #uuid "634e49d5-839b-5c6b-b951-4026df8a6cc8" :answer-field/key "$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449239} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91c4b867-9666-5ae0-9c05-d499cd1aa974" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448400} {:answer/id #uuid "37309c2c-67c4-56cd-985f-06909ed2f0fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37309c2c-67c4-56cd-985f-06909ed2f0fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448401} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0de263f-fa25-53d0-b11f-9d35b7a7944e" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449240} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1c6b3d5-da70-5e84-b633-8354acbdfa7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448402} {:answer/id #uuid "9a074b36-7b55-58d7-9b8f-6233abae5163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a074b36-7b55-58d7-9b8f-6233abae5163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448403} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff92da50-cb51-5769-9d76-97d5131c8ae8" :answer-field/key "$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e8f7202f-949a-5544-b1eb-75cc6a08bdaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-154696" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{1-x},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 1$” is true. Indeed, for $x\\ge 1$, we have $f(x)={e}^{1-x}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{e}^{1-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-{e}^{1-x}]}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =0-(-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function” is true. Since statements “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 1$” and ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$ are true, the first and second conditions are satisfied. So, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 1$”; ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$; “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function”."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193206 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6df4c2be-04e6-571f-b0a9-dfab0dd98141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448466} {:answer/id #uuid "6e49a39a-5368-5de8-8c63-144924ac30d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e49a39a-5368-5de8-8c63-144924ac30d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448467} :answer-field/id #uuid "efee37f6-b4c6-545d-bd91-c2a4f79965cc" :answer-field/key "$\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449273} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a00d201-d27c-52d0-8af0-2805fae6cd94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448468} {:answer/id #uuid "75d7e2d1-7f56-515b-bd02-787ded8e5270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75d7e2d1-7f56-515b-bd02-787ded8e5270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448469} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2b89ba2-4284-58ef-a934-c6ed5ebe6b7d" :answer-field/key "$A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449274} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c6ba7f9-51b0-5600-a4ea-f1ca0eedcc9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448470} {:answer/id #uuid "8af85e9b-1a4d-52a0-b5dc-188c47bd77d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8af85e9b-1a4d-52a0-b5dc-188c47bd77d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448471} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4439f51-b7e9-58c6-8132-dce6ead75ac7" :answer-field/key "$∂A⊂A$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "68256c62-7faf-5747-9e8c-b2c67a0a95a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-161444" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 4\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/62/62c410c21e79e1bc688c1ff1a3440a207c62bcad42525bdc93fa2770b76d48bd.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/33/33a4738b642443c2535d347aa53ee2b7b80c800142c4844d94578769118ebfbc.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$ is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A$ is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A.$\n\n- Statement $∂A⊂A$ is true. In this case, the set $A$ contains all of its boundary points, so we must have $∂A⊂A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$; $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A$; $∂A⊂A$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193207 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fe507f3-8527-5f21-a721-634b58840b7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459593} {:answer/id #uuid "77761fd6-0e94-53bd-b8c5-72db41020fb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fe507f3-8527-5f21-a721-634b58840b7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459593} :answer-field/id #uuid "06f87cf3-f603-5eff-95ff-c1bae5dbb25b" :answer-field/key "$A$ contains all of its boundary points." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460663} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2cd58fb-78f4-562a-9420-9f1156d98abf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459595} {:answer/id #uuid "12885778-ec5e-53cd-96b2-b72a20448566" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12885778-ec5e-53cd-96b2-b72a20448566" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459596} :answer-field/id #uuid "08cf2006-06fc-5f94-8e1a-739789d7be8e" :answer-field/key "$A$ contains none of its boundary points." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460664} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "586336b7-1f15-5b0c-a3b2-dad8f88b8daa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459597} {:answer/id #uuid "c8a5df21-20e1-5124-8aa9-f6e537cc7cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8a5df21-20e1-5124-8aa9-f6e537cc7cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459598} :answer-field/id #uuid "882b009d-1a05-565a-aa3f-fa7d9e3e447a" :answer-field/key "$\\text{int}\\,A=A$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1eb8e242-f7cb-5632-a09c-62dcc98103d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-161445" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y<1-x\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/51/5151df3c5b84ffaca9aa16b401cd9364fea10a240d39c9c28d815b36e417b16d.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, the set $A$ contains none of its boundary points. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement “$A$ contains all of its boundary points” is false, while statement “$A$ contains none of its boundary points” is true. As we've seen, $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n\n- Statement $\\text{int}\\,A=A$ is true. Since $A$ contains none of its boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A=A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ contains none of its boundary points”; $\\text{int}\\,A=A$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3fe3596-340e-5155-bed3-cf25f5843d70" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Two-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e86471bd-e6c5-5896-af8e-c48e8e06dc45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459811} {:answer/id #uuid "0ae10483-1250-5ee8-a5b7-56019635b6ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459812}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e86471bd-e6c5-5896-af8e-c48e8e06dc45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459811} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e3e684d-5745-5881-b7a9-0cb4fc6b3e66" :answer-field/key "![Diagram I](images/e7/e79e96039bc2ebbe1787345b90a84e42e7410c0ef500dfac37844bdc1a10e756.svg)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460772} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85ae8346-7f12-51b2-8e39-86863fc03e25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459813} {:answer/id #uuid "2b7012c9-36fc-589a-9d2d-cf11e2f8491f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85ae8346-7f12-51b2-8e39-86863fc03e25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459813} :answer-field/id #uuid "d610899c-18de-528a-ad41-0903543b7fb2" :answer-field/key "![Diagram II](images/76/7655da5d10b7ace907bd613342d576a691451532aaa7564a9e17c1b41a66097b.svg)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460773} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b69ec572-d020-59f8-83a0-b46528d98a32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459815} {:answer/id #uuid "54b24640-ac48-5b77-ab4e-3e63e4bf019d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b69ec572-d020-59f8-83a0-b46528d98a32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459815} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a6b85af-a931-557d-a8f0-f8d4434ad92c" :answer-field/key "![Diagram III](images/f0/f04fb553eed6853549b0aa6c0f03b05e7f69931d8ba1cfaf0cef2486c82820d7.svg)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "028630ef-e1bc-5d42-ac61-af7d94939f1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-34318" :question/problem "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ lies inside given subset. But\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ lies inside given subset. But\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace since in does not contain the zero-vector.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193319 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192782 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a8dbbab-2dea-5fff-86f0-3411f5405ee9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "619d88ad-a1c7-5b3d-8c24-b6a78974ec7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459847} {:answer/id #uuid "543df3d1-82cc-50ab-9e90-e99e3210da09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "619d88ad-a1c7-5b3d-8c24-b6a78974ec7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459847} :answer-field/id #uuid "adf642b5-0d99-5a3e-8aa9-2b628925bcd1" :answer-field/key "![Diagram I](images/6e/6e8c180b3963c096afadfcfe590f8cff8ee3859438aab883ef08fa18d35ed503.svg)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460790} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c18b93bd-bcdd-50e4-afa3-95fee527dc67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459849} {:answer/id #uuid "41400ea3-a328-570f-81df-c3348d63b6e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41400ea3-a328-570f-81df-c3348d63b6e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459850} :answer-field/id #uuid "56504409-bf23-53eb-a559-4baad15a7cde" :answer-field/key "![Diagram II](images/cd/cdc289944ccb8df5531321f0cea6f1cd9e81cc7f194ee13d0db078c47471696a.svg)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460791} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "192fb319-480e-5a3f-bc8b-a5bec8b0c334" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459851} {:answer/id #uuid "e2e8fcbf-9d24-5fd5-ae32-89e8ef38aa0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "192fb319-480e-5a3f-bc8b-a5bec8b0c334" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459851} :answer-field/id #uuid "fca5cf1d-3106-5e01-b467-cfe14762ca39" :answer-field/key "![Diagram III](images/90/902a485b7c5d2c3fd940b06123ee5316fb74b5e2a2d969b7bf0562fb15cf1a58.svg)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99d3ff8e-37f2-5365-be21-7d8861823943" :question/math-academy-id "q-35595" :question/problem "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II represents the origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,,\\,$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram III is not a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagram II only"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193322 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192765 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6044dc-d4a7-5662-a825-3c13e49d198a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets That are Neither Open Nor Closed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c29d621-f793-5227-8c45-cdc973193a1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459853} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e0b353-6c44-5848-84f3-ca21bd8a12f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c29d621-f793-5227-8c45-cdc973193a1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459853} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8c63b21-f398-5ff0-8619-174f250b0c41" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}<4\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460793} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "313ba5ba-4b76-5ce8-89d7-ca607ead8684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459855} {:answer/id #uuid "50f64642-889e-55ea-970c-b9b08f2e4f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "313ba5ba-4b76-5ce8-89d7-ca607ead8684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459855} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5befb55-bf65-517b-b35a-8ddfd6d9c0c0" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }1\\le {x}^{2}\\le 9,\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460794} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ac6fd3d-18ec-57f8-aeb6-c69b06f785dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459857} {:answer/id #uuid "b0d506b5-c66a-532f-bcab-23809b19c567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0d506b5-c66a-532f-bcab-23809b19c567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459858} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6fc26cd-9cd3-5000-bc44-9e01a45d4536" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }0\\le x\\le 2,\\\\\\,1<y<4\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff2c4e7d-b661-5abc-a5ef-3c554262f85c" :question/math-academy-id "q-35862" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second is *closed* since it contains all its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The third is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(0,2).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }0\\le x\\le 2,\\\\\\,1<y<4\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193323 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192765 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6044dc-d4a7-5662-a825-3c13e49d198a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets That are Neither Open Nor Closed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc67ad68-9c4e-5e01-ad45-5e0583b64cfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477247} {:answer/id #uuid "33373e69-3a76-5ea7-a9ae-d867253e930d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33373e69-3a76-5ea7-a9ae-d867253e930d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477248} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a91c133-f09c-5414-8406-55eccccaf1d1" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le x<2,\\\\\\,0<y<2\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477593} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4347200-6d55-5c61-a030-a4ac871e9a91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477249} {:answer/id #uuid "18b92bb6-72b5-59f3-9611-f5668d921023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4347200-6d55-5c61-a030-a4ac871e9a91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477249} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3901499-272d-57fb-ac61-d587813aa8eb" :answer-field/key "${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<2,}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477594} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97ea91e4-3c46-5e6e-a294-087688f785fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477251} {:answer/id #uuid "0aff04e8-7fae-5878-94e2-41f564a32c8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97ea91e4-3c46-5e6e-a294-087688f785fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477251} :answer-field/id #uuid "c67f297c-e6e9-5e72-a41b-b78ae9b4d08e" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2},\\\\\\,-1\\le x\\le 1\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477595}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8166eb05-ff92-507f-8eb2-6ecb8cda44b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-35866" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(0,1).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(1,0).$\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le x<2,\\\\\\,0<y<2\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193377 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7adf2c3e-4a73-5098-ab60-790d637728cf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Open and Closed Sets in the Plane in Set Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a79b038c-fe01-5ebc-866e-1019c3411829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460161} {:answer/id #uuid "7e520372-4f2a-52fe-80ef-779b4c47f5ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e520372-4f2a-52fe-80ef-779b4c47f5ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460162} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f28ff6b-8064-54e6-ac2a-cbfc20687b92" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }0<y<{x}^{2},\\\\\\,0<x<1\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460947} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebc8c9ed-7eb1-530d-8dc2-74b72a9c7683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460163} {:answer/id #uuid "c32fe42f-7550-5e6f-89b8-a8cf262268b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebc8c9ed-7eb1-530d-8dc2-74b72a9c7683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460163} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6c4ff18-793c-5bf5-b61b-dc2775e56694" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }1\\le x\\le 4,\\\\\\,1\\le {y}^{2}\\le 4\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460948} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0832d56b-30c1-5f4f-a9de-e199bf8d8ced" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460165} {:answer/id #uuid "f481190f-e908-5178-98b4-a892e227e637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0832d56b-30c1-5f4f-a9de-e199bf8d8ced" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460165} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1215464-5386-54ed-a118-8ea6861f3e56" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }0\\le x\\le 3,\\\\\\,-1\\le y\\le 1\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a3b9409-39a3-5316-8079-d83249ab7774" :question/math-academy-id "q-71808" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are open?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }0<y<{x}^{2},\\\\\\,0<x<1\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58fbdce5-60b9-5682-a84e-1c8a5077c762" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Horizontal Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9b4c5e4-e959-56e2-a295-57ff1957f4c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459587} {:answer/id #uuid "35bc7942-5cb9-5f6e-a604-01bbc56e91d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35bc7942-5cb9-5f6e-a604-01bbc56e91d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459588} :answer-field/id #uuid "34d30ed0-77d5-5679-93b2-0ea48f44edef" :answer-field/key "The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460660} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6680343a-55ac-5674-af4a-41f728522fe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459589} {:answer/id #uuid "1ea09bb3-fab5-5fa4-9de9-6cc3c6ceedb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6680343a-55ac-5674-af4a-41f728522fe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459589} :answer-field/id #uuid "a56b6579-beea-5a72-b0d9-20005a09ab04" :answer-field/key "The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460661} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88a4ac9d-8c4a-5b91-b0b8-c3f46a3d66a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459591} {:answer/id #uuid "8a3da9de-0754-5c44-b6f9-c2f9e21352c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459592}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88a4ac9d-8c4a-5b91-b0b8-c3f46a3d66a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459591} :answer-field/id #uuid "323a6fc7-1616-52bd-a614-a99166c8c0e4" :answer-field/key "The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at $O.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460662}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9eb2357b-f9d2-552f-8ddb-db8ea02b5422" :question/math-academy-id "q-160481" :question/problem "![](images/b8/b8874b14fa80881e69cb1daee180d2e342a27c5902e66fc75d43622278176b09.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the bottom side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the bottom side.\n\nFor the bottom side, we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)),\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fcfc165548d8174ec0468dce53317fd0c8daa14565852c5e44d6764161723bed.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement “The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis” is true, while statement “The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis” is false. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement “The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at $O.$” is false.\n\n- When $u=1,$ we obtain the point \n$(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $1.$\n\n- When $u=\\sqrt{2},$ we obtain the point \n$(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right),\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSo, the endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198400 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Inverse Function Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c66ee870-a178-50ce-b0b6-bee0f052561c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477165} {:answer/id #uuid "d77b0e97-6ff2-508e-990d-cafbc2af65a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c66ee870-a178-50ce-b0b6-bee0f052561c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477165} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfadb088-e05e-52c4-b616-ce917e21664e" :answer-field/key "The function $f$ is invertible on $U.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477552} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0fd747d-1226-5143-9384-efab32d27405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477167} {:answer/id #uuid "8bfb1988-f8f1-5d4d-84b1-2e67413309f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bfb1988-f8f1-5d4d-84b1-2e67413309f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477168} :answer-field/id #uuid "92ca9ce2-c60a-5c2d-acdc-aeccd86ae95a" :answer-field/key "The inverse ${f}^{-1}$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477553} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ae9351b-f714-5350-bce2-b00cd09b8417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477169} {:answer/id #uuid "07681ead-4cfe-51ac-ac2e-7698656f7dce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ae9351b-f714-5350-bce2-b00cd09b8417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477169} :answer-field/id #uuid "2586519e-6edc-5b01-bbe2-7c7ddb4c8013" :answer-field/key "The derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=-{f}^{′}(a).$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477554}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b7c80401-6b6e-5da5-9047-45e3ded9ecb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-161828" :question/problem "Let $f:U\\to R$ be continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R$ such that $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U.$ Which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “The function $f$ is invertible on $U.$” is false. According to the theorem, the function $f$ must be invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$ which could be smaller than $U.$ \n\nFor example, the function $f(x)={x}^{2}$ satisfies the conditions of the inverse function theorem for $U=(-2,2)$ and $a=1.$ However, $f(x)$ is not invertible on the entire set $U,$ only on a subset of $U.$\n\n- Statement “The inverse ${f}^{-1}$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$” is true. Indeed, the inverse ${f}^{-1}$ must be continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$\n\n- Statement “The derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=-{f}^{′}(a).$” is false. According to the theorem, we must have ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The inverse ${f}^{-1}$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198261 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d6db336-845a-506a-b020-cced1b38e23a" :knowledge-point/title "Using Laws Involving Implications and Equivalences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb8f3b41-f753-50c9-bbcc-8ee014baf22a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459623} {:answer/id #uuid "63945080-8553-53a8-8917-c9ef2adb87bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb8f3b41-f753-50c9-bbcc-8ee014baf22a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459623} :answer-field/id #uuid "43dea65b-7921-5c81-8166-bd24d95730e9" :answer-field/key "If $x\\,|\\,8$ then $x$ is even, or if $x$ is even then $x\\,|\\,8.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460678} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41a796e3-52fd-59b9-b263-1496fc730b02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459625} {:answer/id #uuid "3cedd932-157a-5436-898a-674c4a2cb077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cedd932-157a-5436-898a-674c4a2cb077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459626} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c4099f7-b667-5d8f-9e68-0c94bab6cced" :answer-field/key "If $x\\,|\\,8$ then $x$ is even, and if $x$ is even then $x\\,|\\,8.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460679} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cc173c6-feb6-5c9f-ba80-153873d0800a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459627} {:answer/id #uuid "7d81fde8-4689-53c6-b96b-75b580b59783" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d81fde8-4689-53c6-b96b-75b580b59783" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459628} :answer-field/id #uuid "92b33259-0e10-51b0-9e0c-da44686b7a25" :answer-field/key "$x\\,|\\,8$ if and only if $x$ is even." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e3f1ffb3-6372-5ba4-9ec3-35379749f5f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-171781" :question/problem "Consider the following predicates.\n\n$\\,$ $P(x):$ $x\\,|\\,8.$ $\\,$ $Q(x):$ $x$ is even.\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the biconditional predicate $P(x)⇔Q(x)?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A biconditional expression $P⇔Q≡(P⇒Q)∧(Q⇒P)$ can be read as shown below:\n\n- $P$ if and only if $Q$\n\n- $P$ is equivalent to $Q$\n\n- If $P$ then $Q,$ and if $Q$ then $P$\n\nSo, $A⇔B$ translates into the following sentences:\n\n- $x\\,|\\,8$ if and only if $x$ is even.\n\n- $x\\,|\\,8$ is equivalent to $x$ is even.\n\n- If $x\\,|\\,8$ then $x$ is even, and if $x$ is even then $x\\,|\\,8.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “If $x\\,|\\,8$ then $x$ is even, and if $x$ is even then $x\\,|\\,8.$”; “$x\\,|\\,8$ if and only if $x$ is even” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198721 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa2f2ccb-45b0-5d6a-aa94-ac3eb95c745b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Predicates of More Than One Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "704e020e-d34a-528d-9e9d-8b82289223c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459647} {:answer/id #uuid "f766b986-cfca-589d-b307-c3cfed4a40df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f766b986-cfca-589d-b307-c3cfed4a40df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459648} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8358696-c4dc-5792-a56d-b4cf06cd1f4e" :answer-field/key "$x<y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460690} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f32f7ace-9473-5adf-87d1-3a0754c1f4bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459649} {:answer/id #uuid "5764e144-7231-5741-8902-987088781ec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5764e144-7231-5741-8902-987088781ec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459650} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c262398-01aa-5334-93fb-2d94d63b0e3f" :answer-field/key "$n$ is a perfect square." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460691} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0937afd-eb79-5420-8b9b-1cc18cc13046" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459651} {:answer/id #uuid "971d785f-c08d-59dc-8767-b018db2c3a8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0937afd-eb79-5420-8b9b-1cc18cc13046" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459651} :answer-field/id #uuid "45cf6747-f781-581e-9df2-b7717fa5597b" :answer-field/key "Are $n$ and $m$ coprime?" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d3a9664a-054f-5dcf-9b0e-e1b4ab3dd171" :question/math-academy-id "q-174456" :question/problem "Which of the following are mathematical predicates?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A *predicate* of the variables ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is a sentence that contains the variables defined on some *universal set*, and has the property that when we substitute concrete values for all the variables into the predicate, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our options. \n\n- Option $x<y$ is a predicate of two variables. Substituting a concrete pair of values $(x,y)$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option “$n$ is a perfect square” is a predicate of one variable. Substituting a concrete value for $n$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option “Are $n$ and $m$ coprime?” is *not* a predicate. Substituting a concrete pair of values $(n,m)$ into the sentence, we do not get a statement that is either true or false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x<y$; “$n$ is a perfect square” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198749 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4a5d6b1-e76a-5b33-8973-04d1d9062dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Orthogonality of a Two-Vector Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6dfedbf-e8f5-5c35-ac7a-3280352e020a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459653} {:answer/id #uuid "e9713a4a-6827-5ab4-8250-e0c6662fd257" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9713a4a-6827-5ab4-8250-e0c6662fd257" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459654} :answer-field/id #uuid "a72b8fb7-3248-5357-886c-14a9921e2b27" :answer-field/key "${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460693} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37b51687-e975-5fef-b586-123a8d03457c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459655} {:answer/id #uuid "4719370a-40fe-5819-95db-2750f3e9f8ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4719370a-40fe-5819-95db-2750f3e9f8ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459656} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a2ed651-b0b2-5578-8e7f-41bdc35291ec" :answer-field/key "${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthogonal" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460694} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb6b8dbf-9094-5908-9192-5e790c2e9227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459657} {:answer/id #uuid "429f461b-d81f-54c3-bf90-863669545081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb6b8dbf-9094-5908-9192-5e790c2e9227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459657} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fb97e7e-61b1-52f5-b06e-85a2025b70ce" :answer-field/key "${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthonormal" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "70a4471c-4be9-5243-a617-e32ac5259d39" :question/math-academy-id "q-177075" :question/problem "$$\n{u}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\,{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=18-18=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthogonal” is true. Indeed, $Q({u}_{1})⊥Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthonormal” is false. Notice that, for example, $Q({u}_{1})$ is not a unit vector since\n\n$$\n‖Q({u}_{1})‖=‖{u}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{{9}^{2}+{6}^{2}}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$; “${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthogonal” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198750 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4a5d6b1-e76a-5b33-8973-04d1d9062dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Orthogonality of a Two-Vector Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dbc2877-1064-59e1-91d9-6e3ec0e08661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459659} {:answer/id #uuid "96fa23cc-f15c-5d76-81ac-bc08e6bb7e90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459660}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96fa23cc-f15c-5d76-81ac-bc08e6bb7e90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459660} :answer-field/id #uuid "17e2d61d-61b6-527d-afb9-3443e06829eb" :answer-field/key "${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460696} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "298a33f1-a9b2-595a-9ecb-d624448c5ff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459661} {:answer/id #uuid "a83a64fc-53ec-556f-b18a-e94d5ea6215f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a83a64fc-53ec-556f-b18a-e94d5ea6215f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459662} :answer-field/id #uuid "18b4689d-0aaf-5f9c-af23-436c85c48526" :answer-field/key "${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthogonal" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460697} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd291524-b945-53dc-9025-02857a4bfebe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459663} {:answer/id #uuid "5ac8bba8-265c-5892-93e3-19c51777ca3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ac8bba8-265c-5892-93e3-19c51777ca3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459664} :answer-field/id #uuid "30a33278-fdbc-523a-bfa4-2f8a09609b7d" :answer-field/key "${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthonormal" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460698}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6d806a7-8383-51f7-a460-be3b003d424b" :question/math-academy-id "q-177315" :question/problem "$$\n{u}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthogonal” is true. Indeed, $Q({u}_{1})⊥Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthonormal” is true. Indeed, both $Q({u}_{1})$ and $Q({u}_{2})$ are unit vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q({u}_{1})‖ & =‖{u}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{\\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)^{2}+\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)^{2}}=1 \\\\ ‖Q({u}_{2})‖ & =‖{u}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)^{2}+\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$; “${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthogonal”; “${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthonormal”."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198784 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198269 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "efdb77bf-dd8b-5ccc-8265-79bd319afaa3" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e6dd7dd-a9af-5b67-8bc5-b3e3616d5b8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459687} {:answer/id #uuid "875c6a37-c169-53f0-bcaf-d71179240c25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "875c6a37-c169-53f0-bcaf-d71179240c25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459688} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bc99ce9-0abe-5b12-a906-54691a59cac2" :answer-field/key "$A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,{n}^{2}-5n+6>0}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460710} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ebff733-5e48-5f0d-8c7d-7d87c5b28ce5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459689} {:answer/id #uuid "954c6b9e-d5a5-5abe-a691-9554db80b606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ebff733-5e48-5f0d-8c7d-7d87c5b28ce5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459689} :answer-field/id #uuid "48eeff13-aa1f-5016-983a-eb5eff08fa76" :answer-field/key "$B={m\\in Z\\,:\\,{m}^{2}-5m+6<0}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460711} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00634cba-72e7-5c17-a0da-5f682bf47501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459691} {:answer/id #uuid "bb8ac5ea-9483-54c7-866a-2654e9f34e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb8ac5ea-9483-54c7-866a-2654e9f34e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459692} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c08c6f8-83b9-5897-a1d0-d8e61df77b44" :answer-field/key "$C={q\\in Q\\,:\\,{q}^{2}-5q+6<0}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e9482778-6f45-5e93-913d-289acb526444" :question/math-academy-id "q-181536" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y={x}^{2}-5x+6$ for $x\\in R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-5x+6 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=2$ and $x=3,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/0a/0a42815f626652c332862a38a3812463a9407605329bd81df25f11274b26ce6f.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality ${x}^{2}-5x+6>0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\nx<2\\,\\text{or}\\,x>3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{1}=(-\\infty ,2)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality ${x}^{2}-5x+6<0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\n2<x<3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{2}=(2,3).$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality ${n}^{2}-5n+6>0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{1}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={…,0,1}\\cup {4,5,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. If $m\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality ${m}^{2}-5m+6<0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to ${q}^{2}-5q+6<0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198270 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f98332fd-e902-5390-a4d3-4e181ce97219" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Trigonometric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18dee266-98ce-54c1-aeac-12e36d53c9f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459693} {:answer/id #uuid "9cfad166-e1d5-5576-97fc-d582dfcd13b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18dee266-98ce-54c1-aeac-12e36d53c9f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459693} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fd83230-91f8-5fa6-922e-bf8570620d34" :answer-field/key "$A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2n\\pi )=1}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460713} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "382ef46e-a95e-5d40-a99d-679cb662d3a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459695} {:answer/id #uuid "eab8131c-ba52-5510-9ce0-7a446f777dcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459696}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eab8131c-ba52-5510-9ce0-7a446f777dcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459696} :answer-field/id #uuid "14b4259e-8704-56d4-8467-5956a49e9711" :answer-field/key "$B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2q\\pi )=1}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460714} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21169332-e448-5bc4-82aa-9564cff51482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459697} {:answer/id #uuid "5547c0e5-4726-5d07-969c-a5ef5603969d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5547c0e5-4726-5d07-969c-a5ef5603969d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459698} :answer-field/id #uuid "56b949af-bf05-5158-8cb8-5296b286a6e6" :answer-field/key "$C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x\\pi )=1}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4e83b937-f1ab-5064-ab2b-f81fab22743d" :question/math-academy-id "q-181597" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nWe start by finding the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x\\pi )=1$ for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\pi  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2m\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{4}+m,\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. There are no values of $m\\in Z$ such that $\\frac{1}{4}+m\\in Z.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $B$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nB={…,-\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{9}{4},…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊆C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198787 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198271 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26758016-ed47-562a-9338-85c71ffbeaa9" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8f53bd8-9ef9-57f4-8a63-cec3891f5e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459699} {:answer/id #uuid "1ffae41e-b8f8-54f4-bbf2-3f0a2cc9c3a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8f53bd8-9ef9-57f4-8a63-cec3891f5e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459699} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a2266ca-93a8-5492-80b5-97a981aaf710" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}A=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({x}^{2})\\,:\\\\\\,n\\in {N}_{0}\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460716} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b29a5306-4144-5f79-908a-f8feff4ba33a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459701} {:answer/id #uuid "03e9eb1b-02d8-506f-82f1-512dd85f1977" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03e9eb1b-02d8-506f-82f1-512dd85f1977" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459702} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cfa2ff5-23d6-55fb-acac-2e57ae1265d5" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}B=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\,:\\\\\\,n\\in {N}_{0}\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460717} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ad9d171-6dd3-541c-afb8-e1779bf6036a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459703} {:answer/id #uuid "d7e2da5c-32be-56a9-8008-4e08536eb809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459704}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7e2da5c-32be-56a9-8008-4e08536eb809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459704} :answer-field/id #uuid "a85a6640-66d8-5a27-8abe-3ec0ab8d48ff" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}C=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\sqrt{x})\\,:\\\\\\,n\\in {N}_{0}\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e02e6bd4-a148-57c6-a612-60228a140b9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-181599" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\n- The set $A$ is finite. Listing a few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})=2x \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)=2 \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2)=0 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(0)=0 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, for $n>3,$ we have ${f}^{(n)}(x)=0.$ Listing a few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\nA={{x}^{2},2x,2,0}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=4,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nB={\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\\frac{1}{x},-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Similar to the previous case, listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}} \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}\\right)=\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nC={\\sqrt{x},\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}},-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}},\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198797 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198269 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "efdb77bf-dd8b-5ccc-8265-79bd319afaa3" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8709e44f-9eb1-5aa6-bcfe-43cf3fc3faf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462211} {:answer/id #uuid "27d5ab67-21dd-5ddf-963d-a807ba7fa6c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27d5ab67-21dd-5ddf-963d-a807ba7fa6c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462212} :answer-field/id #uuid "36cba5d5-c85e-5904-a5c8-298686c30fc8" :answer-field/key "$A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,|2n|>5}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462859} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e8c7c96-b1cc-5dde-a9a0-abbbbc82ee3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462213} {:answer/id #uuid "026c8874-89cb-59ed-b68e-4121b69a008f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "026c8874-89cb-59ed-b68e-4121b69a008f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462214} :answer-field/id #uuid "908ef17b-a642-5b4a-a3a3-f8a1c602221f" :answer-field/key "$B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,|2q|>5}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462860} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ae0ddd9-724e-56a2-bcab-8344c45a63ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462215} {:answer/id #uuid "dee8c65a-8131-573c-a2b7-3b5c4032f90f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dee8c65a-8131-573c-a2b7-3b5c4032f90f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462216} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b6d889c-fee7-5bfa-bcf5-7ab3278080c2" :answer-field/key "$C={x\\in R\\,:\\,|2x|>5}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "16d5f3be-e7a0-5c29-a089-f858318b66bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-181722" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nWe start by solving the following inequality for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2x|>5 \\\\ 2|x|>5 \\\\ |x|>\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nx<-\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{or}\\,x>\\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in I=(-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{2})\\cup (\\frac{5}{2},\\infty ).$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $|2n|>5$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nA={…,-4,-3}\\cup {3,4,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $|2q|>5$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊂C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,B,$ and $C.$\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198859 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198283 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c26aeaa-9252-5bd3-9ad6-0fc25ca6b03c" :knowledge-point/title "Necessary and Sufficient Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36f3fd87-f83d-5f24-9a9c-2f1e10b494f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462217} {:answer/id #uuid "49c73b7a-7a95-579d-9ed4-ed7f399c4ba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49c73b7a-7a95-579d-9ed4-ed7f399c4ba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462218} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff59416c-f6a2-5db0-b15e-fdd93adea941" :answer-field/key "$6\\,|\\,x$ is sufficient for $3\\,|\\,x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462862} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e26dd87-222d-53c2-b445-02b6c273d202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462219} {:answer/id #uuid "99c008c3-91fd-5472-8d4c-0c81d6a0763e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99c008c3-91fd-5472-8d4c-0c81d6a0763e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462220} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ff83462-5be9-5b17-9714-db25c409d85a" :answer-field/key "For $3\\,|\\,x,$ it is sufficient that $6\\,|\\,x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462863} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "647e34ca-f77c-50e8-873d-b3b07431819c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462221} {:answer/id #uuid "0c51f730-21dd-5e1c-815e-a111ba00fff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "647e34ca-f77c-50e8-873d-b3b07431819c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462221} :answer-field/id #uuid "d30794bc-281a-5d41-943b-b8c998897fa2" :answer-field/key "$6\\,|\\,x$ is necessary for $3\\,|\\,x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462864} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b92dd1c2-7470-51b7-84bd-43a97469e0ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462223} {:answer/id #uuid "7d8c081b-8a77-5970-9a81-d525e92f384c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d8c081b-8a77-5970-9a81-d525e92f384c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462224} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa0501cc-b8f8-5bbe-b3f3-b801954fc967" :answer-field/key "For $6\\,|\\,x,$ it is necessary that $3\\,|\\,x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "587a7a92-f47b-5043-840d-bba7363fe179" :question/math-academy-id "q-183495" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(x):$ $6\\,|\\,x$ $\\,$ $Q(x):$ $3\\,|\\,x$\n\nConsider the predicates $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ above. Which of the following expresses the conditional predicate $P(x)⇒Q(x)?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe have the following grammatical constructions for $S⇒N:$\n\n- $S$ is sufficient for $N$\n\n- For $N,$ it is sufficient that $S$\n\n- $N$ is necessary for $S$\n\n- For $S,$ it is necessary that $N$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option “$6\\,|\\,x$ is sufficient for $3\\,|\\,x$” reads\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{6\\,|\\,x}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{is sufficient for}\\,\\underbrace{3\\,|\\,x}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Option “For $3\\,|\\,x,$ it is sufficient that $6\\,|\\,x$” reads\n\n$$\n\\text{for}\\,\\underbrace{3\\,|\\,x,}_{Q(x)}\\,\\text{it is sufficient that}\\,\\underbrace{6\\,|\\,x}_{P(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Option “$6\\,|\\,x$ is necessary for $3\\,|\\,x$” reads\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{6\\,|\\,x}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{is necessary for}\\,\\underbrace{3\\,|\\,x}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x)⇒P(x).$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- Option “For $6\\,|\\,x,$ it is necessary that $3\\,|\\,x$” reads\n\n$$\n\\text{for}\\,\\underbrace{6\\,|\\,x,}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{it is necessary that}\\,\\underbrace{3\\,|\\,x}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$6\\,|\\,x$ is sufficient for $3\\,|\\,x$”; “For $3\\,|\\,x,$ it is sufficient that $6\\,|\\,x$”; “For $6\\,|\\,x,$ it is necessary that $3\\,|\\,x$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b3101aa-ee9e-5940-b170-2f4e78802150" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Divisibility of a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83cc7232-5969-5d3c-a119-b15eb4215b8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448780} {:answer/id #uuid "ff412847-0716-514c-8536-d1ee843c1fc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff412847-0716-514c-8536-d1ee843c1fc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448781} :answer-field/id #uuid "b51784ef-d8c9-5ec9-b0a0-30e904788e60" :answer-field/key "If $15\\,|\\,32b$ then $15\\,|\\,b$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449430} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "daaebca9-b9ad-53d2-b86b-f95064bbda79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448782} {:answer/id #uuid "92cdfcd5-cda5-5399-9e66-30a8ed9d661a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "daaebca9-b9ad-53d2-b86b-f95064bbda79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448782} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b1825e4-5bd6-5f87-b096-192d4860b495" :answer-field/key "If ${7}^{2}\\,|\\,7b$ then ${7}^{2}\\,|\\,b$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449431} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eedbbda7-a151-5d3a-8d63-8745040bf202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448784} {:answer/id #uuid "0c82ae71-f1c3-5324-b48d-9b827278acfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c82ae71-f1c3-5324-b48d-9b827278acfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448785} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9c39e4f-89f1-5ba1-8177-6dbaa5f29af2" :answer-field/key "If ${3}^{2}\\,|\\,25b$ then $9\\,|\\,b$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f6ff943b-6246-50f5-830d-737c87bafa4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-92248" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$\n\n$c\\,|\\,ab\\,$ and $\\,\\text{gcd}(a,c)=1$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $\\,c\\,|\\,b.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement “If $15\\,|\\,32b$ then $15\\,|\\,b$” is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(15,32)=1.$ Therefore, we have $15\\,|\\,32b$ and $\\text{gcd}(15,32)=1,$ which implies $15\\,|\\,b.$\n\n- Statement “If ${7}^{2}\\,|\\,7b$ then ${7}^{2}\\,|\\,b$” is false. As a counterexample, let $b=7.$ Then, ${7}^{2}∣7\\cdot 7$ but ${7}^{2}∤7.$\n\n- Statement “If ${3}^{2}\\,|\\,25b$ then $9\\,|\\,b$” is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}({3}^{2},25)=1.$ Therefore, we have ${3}^{2}∣25b$ and $\\text{gcd}({3}^{2},25)=1,$ which implies ${3}^{2}\\,|\\,b.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “If $15\\,|\\,32b$ then $15\\,|\\,b$”; “If ${3}^{2}\\,|\\,25b$ then $9\\,|\\,b$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "220647d1-71b8-5d0a-b497-e5d3ad7b6da2" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Graphs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b91f3c08-8c48-5112-9ed4-298a23417e39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462225} {:answer/id #uuid "4eb48aee-165f-553f-943a-1dc1a93a1ba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4eb48aee-165f-553f-943a-1dc1a93a1ba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462226} :answer-field/id #uuid "95d9122a-4241-5d15-911c-ee537ed4c9d2" :answer-field/key "$(2,♠)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462866} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8bea4de-24a6-51d3-9d13-9aec0ac73d11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462227} {:answer/id #uuid "215cb620-ff23-54df-9c49-5769271739ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8bea4de-24a6-51d3-9d13-9aec0ac73d11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462227} :answer-field/id #uuid "a424a42b-0971-57df-b20a-9729c34079ef" :answer-field/key "$(3,♣)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462867} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4b97ac9-352c-5fb4-8ec4-f180d08d2c48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462229} {:answer/id #uuid "247d398a-1b3a-5517-a755-c5805f82cc29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "247d398a-1b3a-5517-a755-c5805f82cc29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462230} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c38dc07-5d66-5619-a22f-f60f5317be94" :answer-field/key "$(4,♣)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e5c1c70-9337-53c9-94bd-f7f57b8f63c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-212112" :question/problem "![](images/01/015cb5b350ad2692c1371d20c367e6c01a81b981351ba33b61e6c95854862d63.png)\n\nThe plot of the binary relation $S,$ defined over the sets $X=\\{2,3,4\\}$ and $Y=\\{♣,♠\\},$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a plot (or graph), where \n\n- the first set is plotted along the horizontal axis,\n\n- the second set is plotted along the vertical axis, and\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines a point with coordinates $(x,y)$ on the plot.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(2,♠)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the plot contains a point with these coordinates.\n\n- The pair $(3,♣)$ does not belong to the relation since the plot does not contain a point with such coordinates.\n\n- The pair $(4,♣)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the plot contains a point with these coordinates.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $(2,♠)$; $(4,♣)$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e9c4fd-dae7-5858-b008-ca0e144fa64a" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Digraphs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdf7c76b-8b6e-5119-a86f-374f60a22420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462231} {:answer/id #uuid "c3e7ccd7-6400-5840-91a0-4f6de82a26ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3e7ccd7-6400-5840-91a0-4f6de82a26ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462232} :answer-field/id #uuid "54b56440-323e-5a26-9f08-7893c5738506" :answer-field/key "$(2,1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462869} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49daca3b-d45a-5a14-aec9-dd7d44925964" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462233} {:answer/id #uuid "db2a60f2-11ae-55a0-b384-24f744f26987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db2a60f2-11ae-55a0-b384-24f744f26987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462234} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec3276d5-569e-5fbd-ba45-c2fe6da73cd5" :answer-field/key "$(3,3)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462870} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18f9fa29-a41d-5885-9342-2d80a73cceeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462235} {:answer/id #uuid "4cae6d85-a5b2-5a4b-b347-4d679539168e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18f9fa29-a41d-5885-9342-2d80a73cceeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462235} :answer-field/id #uuid "875acd4d-724c-54d0-9aff-9f9aff84a49a" :answer-field/key "$(2,3)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc05772e-ea0c-5a4b-9ddc-8e7c8974bf6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-212120" :question/problem "![](images/84/847bcee3b9f9fc6672d5858111866141e043e390605cd92fb5579fd5193d7df4.png)\n\nThe directed graph of a relation defined on the set $\\{1,2,3\\}$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a directed graph (digraph), where \n\n- the nodes (vertices) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an arrow from node $x$ to node $y$,\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a loop from node $x$ to itself.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(2,1)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has an arrow that starts at the node $2$ and ends at the node $1.$\n\n- The pair $(3,3)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has a loop from the node $3$ to itself.\n\n- The pair $(2,3)$ does not belong to the relation since the directed graph does not have an arrow that starts at the node $2$ and ends at the node $3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $(2,1)$; $(3,3)$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204256 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4dee31d-47c2-5b56-8cea-22d42f7b9de2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric and Antisymmetric Relations From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f991e8f8-3549-51b7-8f46-a4f179c9e60c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462237} {:answer/id #uuid "430e2dd1-7999-5f15-afe8-311016bcad2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "430e2dd1-7999-5f15-afe8-311016bcad2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462238} :answer-field/id #uuid "350403e0-9726-5cab-b456-3642d421c3e4" :answer-field/key "![Plot I](images/72/72ce19aec6251ebe580f3b3c2368c1d56d020d547c4e2fb78b33cf3bfc6f9e9e.svg)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462872} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41c6b181-ec83-5978-b96f-c05bd5fa6026" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462239} {:answer/id #uuid "450492a6-bd5c-51c7-b747-45d35fdc4a6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462240}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41c6b181-ec83-5978-b96f-c05bd5fa6026" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462239} :answer-field/id #uuid "be16f79d-a666-5425-8685-2229c8dc678d" :answer-field/key "![Plot II](images/ca/cabd107b8ba19060094506c0e58e37ae1e936f49e4ff93f7ca81acbc81fe46b6.svg)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462873} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d3452bf-da3c-501c-9084-9b3c0d559be9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462241} {:answer/id #uuid "d82344f8-5918-5907-a948-a6d6d2add8cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d3452bf-da3c-501c-9084-9b3c0d559be9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462241} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9e9a683-a17d-579c-833c-8bcb7bc62ef7" :answer-field/key "![Plot III](images/0c/0cbce0239db07380cd4ee60451a550c40edc82f1c7bba6e4f90a65091d289e7f.svg)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8e6d9019-7f0e-55d6-8ba5-3c61199bf5af" :question/math-academy-id "q-213311" :question/problem "Which of the plots below represent symmetric relations defined on the set $A={0,2,4}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a plot, if a point $(x,y)$ belongs to the plot, then $(y,x),$ the point symmetric to $(x,y)$ over the diagonal line $y=x,$ should also be on the plot.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the points, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be on the plot of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows points on the diagonal line $y=x.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's draw the diagonal line $y=x$ and examine our plots.\n\n![](images/9c/9c9c16300cfb95a61a261e39db865d5c6d056794bd7aaa422567bde10dd4abf5.png)\n\n \n\n- Plot I represents a symmetric relation since the plot is symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\n- Plot II represents an antisymmetric relation since the plot contains at most one point from each pair of the points symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\n- Plot III represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since, for example, the pair $(4,2)$ belongs to the plot while the point $(2,4)$ does not. And it's not antisymmetric since it contains the pairs $(0,4)$ and $(4,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is plot I only"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204600 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6226fdb-ee73-5a78-87d5-9d6a988a9eb4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation With No Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07ccdaed-3b16-539d-8f3a-f28aba00eadc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439621} {:answer/id #uuid "51c238ba-1654-57e5-8062-656c1bcfb26e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07ccdaed-3b16-539d-8f3a-f28aba00eadc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439621} :answer-field/id #uuid "db08f348-7240-5e25-aac6-71acb1cc27dd" :answer-field/key "$2x = x + 1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440168} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c882365-82b2-5f84-84e5-7313f81ee537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439623} {:answer/id #uuid "aa39a00f-0662-511a-8732-c1dcb22cb653" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c882365-82b2-5f84-84